    "title": "How to watch the Artemis II return to earth live",
    "text": "One Northeast Ohio doctor says the crisis in Iran is no longer something she watches from afar — it's something she's living through every day. CLEVELAND — As tensions escalate between the United States and Iran, one Northeast Ohio doctor says the crisis is no longer something she watches from afar — it's something she's living through every day. Simin, who asked that her last name not be used to protect her family, immigrated to the U.S. about 40 years ago to study medicine. She's spent nearly three decades working in Northeast Ohio, but now, her focus is on family members still in Iran — and the limited contact she has with them. \"They've been in complete digital shutdown,\" Simin said. \"Every few days, they call and let us know that they're OK.\" She says those calls are brief and infrequent, adding to the uncertainty and fear she feels as the situation overseas intensifies. Simin describes conditions in Iran as increasingly dangerous, saying many people feel they have little ability to safely speak out. \"Any peaceful demonstration was shut down with the highest brutality,\" she told 3News. At the same time, rhetoric from President Donald Trump has escalated in recent days, including a social media post warning that \"a whole civilization will die\" as tensions rise over Iran's actions in the Strait of Hormuz. Simin says many in the Iranian community are desperate for change, but worried about the cost. \"It's horrible. I wake up several times hoping ... this regime ends,\" she said. \"We want the U.S. and Israel to continue to target IRGC (the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps).\" Still, she draws a clear line when it comes to civilian safety. \"We're asking President Trump not to target civilian infrastructure,\" Simin declared. \"No electricity and no water for people, and this is very dangerous.\" She emphasized that while many want to see change in Iran's leadership, they do not want to see civilians harmed in the process. \"Nobody wants war. We don't want to be bombed,\" she said. \"We're afraid for American soldiers now to be involved.\" Her message to people here in the U.S. is simple: pay attention and remember the human impact behind the headlines. \"Keep the pressure on them,\" she said, \"but save and protect civilians.\" For now, she says her biggest concern remains the safety of her loved ones, and hoping that each time the phone rings, it brings good news.",
